[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Interfaces, Default Methods, and Multiple Inheritance Resolution in Java"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"Prior to Java 8, interfaces were strictly contracts containing abstract method signatures and constant variables. When an interface changed, all implementing classes were forced to implement the new methods. Java 8 introduced default methods to allow developers to add new functionality to existing interfaces without breaking legacy code. However, this introduced a form of multiple inheritance, requiring explicit rules to resolve conflicts when a class implements multiple interfaces with identical default methods."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Default Methods"},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"A default method is declared inside an interface using the 'default' keyword. It includes a body, enabling classes that implement the interface to inherit the method implementation automatically without writing it themselves."},{"order":6,"type":"code","text":"public interface Vehicle {
    void start();

    default void fuelUp() {
        System.out.println("Filling up with standard fuel...");
    }
}"},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"The Diamond Problem and Multiple Inheritance Resolution"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"When a class implements multiple interfaces that contain default methods with the exact same signature, Java faces the diamond problem. The compiler cannot automatically decide which implementation to inherit and will throw a compilation error unless the ambiguity is explicitly resolved by the implementing class."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"public interface Engine {
    default void diagnose() {
        System.out.println("Engine diagnostics running...");
    }
}

public interface ElectricalSystem {
    default void diagnose() {
        System.out.println("Electrical diagnostics running...");
    }
}

public class HybridCar implements Engine, ElectricalSystem {
    // Compilation Error if diagnose() is not overridden!
}"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Rules for Resolving Conflicts"},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Rule 1: Classes Win. If a class inherits a method from a superclass and a default method from an interface with the same signature, the superclass method wins completely, ignoring the interface default method."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Rule 2: Most Specific Interface Wins. If two interfaces inherit from one another, the more specific interface's default method takes precedence."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Rule 3: Explicit Override Required. If unrelated interfaces provide conflicting default methods, the implementing class must explicitly override the method and choose which implementation to invoke using the 'Super' syntax."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Practical Implementation"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here is how you explicitly resolve a conflict between two unrelated interfaces by invoking a specific interface's default method using 'InterfaceName.super.methodName()'."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"public class HybridCar implements Engine, ElectricalSystem {
    @Override
    public void diagnose() {
        // Explicitly choose Engine's default implementation
        Engine.super.diagnose();

        // Or combine both, or write custom logic
        ElectricalSystem.super.diagnose();

        System.out.println("Hybrid car comprehensive diagnostics complete.");
    }
}"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Default methods provide immense flexibility for evolving APIs in Java without breaking backwards compatibility. By understanding the strict resolution rules (Classes over Interfaces, Sub-interfaces over Super-interfaces, and explicit super calls for unrelated conflicts), developers can safely leverage multiple interface inheritance without ambiguity."}]
